#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124344 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "8914449a-3d6f-5847-9e94-3be0a2ccbc94" :question/math-academy-id "e-9178" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/859a831dcc11fa6ffe430cc913fa46283d3231816429d3fea98219fe3417b746.png)\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n\n- $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/a172ba62aed6c25fc1b40fdf70b1ae88a7e33d2f3341696783b96defd927b6be.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124345 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d8798f55-f005-5019-bf47-349e43237888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9179" :question/problem "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Laptop** | **Desktop** |\n| To: | **Laptop** | $0.9$ | $0.8$ |\n| **Desktop** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase. \n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/1935c4960a48e2b446c2610b9edc2a160a5f45462494c72a90f139ea2d13cc34.png)\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/29b5a209bb27b24f165ee418326763d8fb24f83f2b3a767052c381aa8cd0e01c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=8{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/0e8f620c3392dbb77b1353184e38eaf48b526aa82c502772a5582bd20c7f5aea.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/2ed7b0fc7296b2b651909c7c15c73e253fc8a9f95fb037072228d5de6f838894.png)\n\nSo, ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}$ of the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2164d0ba-43d5-53c7-adf1-9e6810b316a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9180" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/093de94431946cb83e7cc0523405989b7a23cc8d9677bdecdfba7aaf207fd62b.png)\n\nFind the steady-state vector $\\mathbf q$ of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/5ed2f76d9b2857134a5b2aa5781711f9625cabb46a9ca3c3d54aa31c32bb4dbe.png)\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=20{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=20{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+9{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 57 & -19 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2x_1 -7\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\\right) + x_3 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/9ae5533dc825d2d3d08ca7a54c50578e21dda8fad7988b9043baaad2a69368b7.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/fd7775b5d982d0bc51e80417a70b67dbff0176afcc5ff9aec613947076201386.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432478a8-bad6-5f9f-a515-4b0cf66d97e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121148} {:answer/id #uuid "52af6a52-6f24-59d0-a860-07c22969f81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121149} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0cde97-4842-5f30-a5d4-d6572633263c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121150} {:answer/id #uuid "63574b9e-c5f6-527e-8f99-288e3a17bc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121151} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8fe656-cd56-533b-9eda-08ac3657a873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63574b9e-c5f6-527e-8f99-288e3a17bc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121151} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5c9b48-0796-5549-a9b4-9a879bcd30f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3662430d-84f0-5632-b066-9a7b273fafeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110189" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.4}{15}+\\frac{5.6}{15} \\\\ \\frac{5.6}{15}+\\frac{2.4}{15}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82b4c7a0-e195-5c5e-919a-3112031d5ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121154} {:answer/id #uuid "9020e449-fe74-5614-836e-b46ada002820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121155} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ad6b11-a781-53e8-9f82-2f95ace10781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121156} {:answer/id #uuid "13dd5d4a-536a-54a9-88b5-c10652537dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121157} {:answer/id #uuid "3199d054-4549-5945-b935-7f80fa13239e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3199d054-4549-5945-b935-7f80fa13239e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121158} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b80cc76-1931-5799-8618-9b5bc1056106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d2ea1fa-42c3-5215-bede-8b745015a967" :question/math-academy-id "q-110239" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+\\frac{1.5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1.5}{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8393f4e-aea7-56fc-b212-cf8c98502cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121160} {:answer/id #uuid "af5dda9e-fd3b-5334-82b6-2b657a97aa53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121161} {:answer/id #uuid "e24438a0-da2a-5e68-aade-96f44f597920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121162} {:answer/id #uuid "15b7850b-fe3a-51fd-b0da-e97fd832fffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121163} {:answer/id #uuid "72e5e789-03fc-5c09-b2f6-519cdc03a1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8393f4e-aea7-56fc-b212-cf8c98502cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121160} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d51feb7-ac17-54bd-b65a-a4fa5fc15ed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "496d0736-5684-529d-8dd0-8b061c9a6089" :question/math-academy-id "q-110242" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "996354da-ffa1-5f66-825e-85c1fc82506e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342226} {:answer/id #uuid "1637b7cc-7816-5b20-ab20-fd3ec2553619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342227} {:answer/id #uuid "44511b43-37e7-5a09-80e6-03886e9d2300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342228} {:answer/id #uuid "0517cfb1-d16a-5115-b237-68ca90f4652d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342229} {:answer/id #uuid "0da29330-97e8-5c08-a5b9-e926862ea06e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1637b7cc-7816-5b20-ab20-fd3ec2553619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342227} :answer-field/id #uuid "83cc77b2-be86-5143-9d4f-fe8f63aa2909" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "224b3ef2-275a-5b9b-8943-2770e7619fc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-135306" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.4}{7}+\\frac{0.6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{7}+\\frac{3.4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c124eb-7990-5a51-a5d8-775a785f8439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342233} {:answer/id #uuid "59c8d70e-ba02-5735-ae76-a4555a62fb3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342234} {:answer/id #uuid "15bf75da-16aa-5633-bbeb-7a69b38b6e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342235} {:answer/id #uuid "4d461c17-b58e-5023-8c3a-c2ad4253b2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342236} {:answer/id #uuid "ad117072-e02c-57ec-a98f-4c3d21553667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15bf75da-16aa-5633-bbeb-7a69b38b6e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342235} :answer-field/id #uuid "789ae930-bd06-5d6e-9597-e5a1beb87c1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f14c2161-b221-51e8-9068-326a4f843d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-135310" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.5}{9} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2.4}{9}+\\frac{1.6}{9}+\\frac{0.3}{9}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4.3}{9}\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a600d558-c1f7-5011-b7e5-7dcde2682dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342240} {:answer/id #uuid "d689dc5d-6fab-558f-ab85-e04cf8370ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342241} {:answer/id #uuid "409dba80-03f0-5d5a-81bb-bc02d4266b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342242} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1447be-964c-502c-8c2b-8636dc6c2f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342243} {:answer/id #uuid "bef183b8-2db6-56f1-ac95-f87f184c696f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "409dba80-03f0-5d5a-81bb-bc02d4266b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342242} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c9abf1c-6664-5535-9fcf-917573dbb6cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "356faffb-7793-5fb7-ac0b-96c95b3bd6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-135311" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.03+0.105+0.12 \\\\ 0.06+0.49+0.07 \\\\ 0.01+0.105+0.01\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.255 \\\\ 0.62 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0b87a9-d299-5a56-8a6a-22d820d2115e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342247} {:answer/id #uuid "347fcef8-781e-572f-806b-5dd979ef962c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342248} {:answer/id #uuid "6df85110-15fd-505e-b5ac-92393d37976c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342249} {:answer/id #uuid "77063f55-06e8-542e-9b7a-8b8342e80dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342250} {:answer/id #uuid "58b382ab-a56a-5b49-8e9d-787ae8352859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58b382ab-a56a-5b49-8e9d-787ae8352859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342251} :answer-field/id #uuid "b693fe4e-03e4-5e8c-b592-73edca9a4053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "696e69c1-aaed-5b30-adfb-2c3b0b8df1c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-135323" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.75{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 75{x}_{1}-60{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{5}{9}=-\\frac{1}{9}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67851d5-a3dc-5b49-8194-6ed059e0f57f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342254} {:answer/id #uuid "e34f64bb-1040-583b-b296-6e5ec8befb10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342255} {:answer/id #uuid "92e8220f-7ede-5531-90cd-1d34a4c38dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342256} {:answer/id #uuid "76880b0d-414c-5a4d-a217-7d8835529d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342257} {:answer/id #uuid "051716d7-e0b8-583c-a9e2-7686a7203dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "051716d7-e0b8-583c-a9e2-7686a7203dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342258} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf77d19-a620-5f4c-b2c3-617769595dd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fea3684-1d6a-5826-a4b9-83e705fd159c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135325" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & -0.3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{13}{10}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{10}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{13} \\\\ \\frac{10}{13}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{13}-\\frac{10}{13}=-\\frac{7}{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47353adb-d497-5fa5-a49e-7681b857860c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342261} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3182ae-4d4d-5e84-ad0e-0280dc50dc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{33}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342262} {:answer/id #uuid "90f2aee8-5099-55e5-8434-b5bdfed0f2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342263} {:answer/id #uuid "b80213a9-50e7-52a8-9b78-68b1fb760d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{33}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342264} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fc5908-16df-54ed-a2a1-a17730f52f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c3182ae-4d4d-5e84-ad0e-0280dc50dc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{33}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342262} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a306d4d-6c50-5829-8ef7-d3229a2ce859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "493b2715-cbc4-552a-b444-e7807620d6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-135330" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 1 \\\\ 0.65 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 1 \\\\ 0.65 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 1 \\\\ 0.65 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.65 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.65{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 65{x}_{1}-100{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}-20{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{20}{13}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{13}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{13}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{33}{13}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{13}{33}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{33} \\\\ \\frac{13}{33}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{20}{33}-\\frac{13}{33}=\\frac{7}{33}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b72bb34-e9a9-598f-af83-fe4219b80009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342268} {:answer/id #uuid "52f3f8df-99ad-5c0d-845f-dd4c0ad54a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342269} {:answer/id #uuid "a362d0df-43f6-5cbd-877d-ecb47458173b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342270} {:answer/id #uuid "0559f778-e830-5cea-bda4-fec7a3d10398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342271} {:answer/id #uuid "cf98a9d9-03e2-55b0-b975-eea721940572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf98a9d9-03e2-55b0-b975-eea721940572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342272} :answer-field/id #uuid "7918654f-e285-59dc-b13b-635e418f5503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6be156a-2b5e-5033-8af9-c73086a6bd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-135344" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 6.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -7.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+0.5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bed4e822-8907-5378-98ee-1e5f188dfd74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342275} {:answer/id #uuid "a281f01a-da97-51f6-8eed-270aac899700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342276} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5b2ab7-0f38-555f-8755-3a8b4ce94fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342277} {:answer/id #uuid "6bccd713-85e7-5d4e-a554-cb07cb529eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342278} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d6b486-5077-556b-9754-1541d9575e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bccd713-85e7-5d4e-a554-cb07cb529eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342278} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c26d446-a25a-5bf8-9909-7a8bf199311f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4c80da8-397f-5662-ba7f-3c2b3d84443d" :question/math-academy-id "q-135355" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.6 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.9 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12 & -18 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+6(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3}{11} \\\\ \\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{6}{11}+\\frac{3}{11}-\\frac{2}{11}=\\frac{7}{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be0a8077-3b4f-58c5-b172-9dc75ca6a046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342282} {:answer/id #uuid "f09fd2c2-f4f5-5264-9bed-c09048ffa335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342283} {:answer/id #uuid "47abbc21-177f-5903-a443-c433e6b97303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342284} {:answer/id #uuid "e981bcfb-3dbc-525c-a92d-e8c2dfe5d85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342285} {:answer/id #uuid "e21eea60-096d-5129-9df6-8af9d44aa77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09fd2c2-f4f5-5264-9bed-c09048ffa335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342283} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa075338-57bf-5f22-9c69-021fcc95c24e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05a39ded-7d7a-5e3b-b1de-693f0bc3abbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-135408" :question/problem "There are two groups of people In a small town: those who travel to work by car and those who travel by train. Every year a study is done in which people are asked about their preference between using trains versus cars. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Train** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Train** | $0.65$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $65%$ of people who currently travel by car will switch to train next year, while $25%$ of people who currently travel by train will switch to their car next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $90\\,000$ people that travel to work by train or car, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use the train to travel to work?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{18}{13}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{13}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{13}{18}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{13}{18}$ of the people will eventually use the train to travel. Therefore, the number of people who will eventually use the train is\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{18}\\cdot 90\\,000=65\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d6b4aa-7bdd-5e79-806b-cbd42e4402d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342289} {:answer/id #uuid "6550b82a-eafa-5999-b435-780ca8d5c0ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342290} {:answer/id #uuid "266e89ea-f009-5edc-afde-ef983f41dfce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342291} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc88b31-2545-53e7-8d46-9806ded34741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342292} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e56527-20e1-555c-8978-75aa597c72da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4e56527-20e1-555c-8978-75aa597c72da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342293} :answer-field/id #uuid "803dbf47-bd15-593e-a307-ee40b421c90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f64144f1-4784-509a-8280-15bc282eeef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-135432" :question/problem "A company processes and packages three types of rice: rice $A,$ rice $B,$ and rice $C.$ A study by the company's sales analysts demonstrates how consumer preference changes from one kind of rice to another year after year. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rice $A$** | **Rice $B$** | **Rice $C$** |\n| To: | **Rice $A$** | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| **Rice $B$** | $0.2$ | $0.7$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Rice $C$** | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.65$ |  |\n\nFor example, $20%$ of the people who preferred rice $B$ last year switched to rice $C$ this year.\n\nIf the company has $200\\,000$ customers and the listed probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually purchase rice $B?$" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & -0.35\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.1 & 0.15 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.3 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & -0.35 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=100{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=100{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -30 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 30 & 20 & -35 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -26 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 26 & -26 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -26 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{5}$ of the customers will consume rice $B.$ Therefore, the number of people who will eventually consume rice $B$ will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 200\\,000=80\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12cbb84c-7ab3-5e5c-a2e4-2edcd3ff3a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342296} {:answer/id #uuid "e2908d65-2456-531a-8708-48b21605757a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "340" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342297} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8806cc-31cc-57dc-b7d2-70e41a85781c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342298} {:answer/id #uuid "f1066c6d-cde9-5269-bde3-f99fe8189e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342299} {:answer/id #uuid "5aec5c90-0af0-5d1a-b196-c1e27789708f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aec5c90-0af0-5d1a-b196-c1e27789708f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342300} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4e2351-f2aa-5fa6-a595-16cc169555b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a031dbeb-978c-5542-b4e9-e4e60f2dc984" :question/math-academy-id "q-141024" :question/problem "A study conducted on $3\\,400$ teenagers by the transportation department of a particular city reveals that teenagers' preference for the type of home-to-school transportation changes with time. The following table shows the results of this study.  \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Bus** | **Bicycle** | **Electric Scooter ** |\n| To: | **Bus** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Bicycle** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Electric Scooter** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of teenagers who used to go to school by bus now go by bicycle.  \n\nHow many teenagers would eventually go to school on electric scooters if the listed probabilities remained constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cd4c8d-a940-5ddd-b100-55c57b972ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342303} {:answer/id #uuid "988496d8-e783-5faa-8afc-00a7da709fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342304} {:answer/id #uuid "832636af-5e32-5e8e-a70f-0899d4733c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342305} {:answer/id #uuid "c51165d8-9d04-581d-b131-d4615e6ea7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342306} {:answer/id #uuid "54f07ad4-5fd7-52f2-bb37-ed138b81d121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54f07ad4-5fd7-52f2-bb37-ed138b81d121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342307} :answer-field/id #uuid "94bb32e2-c8af-5d16-8c0a-d4734611d04c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a524bf3-7523-5a68-9c7e-5b4a3b2b91b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141035" :question/problem "A study shows how the sports preferences of young people in a certain community vary from year to year. The following table shows the results of this study. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Baseball** | **Basketball** | **Football** |\n| To: | **Baseball** | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Basketball** | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Football** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of people who used to prefer baseball now prefer football. \n\nGiven that $1\\,260$ young people were interviewed for the study and that the probabilities listed remain constant for many years, how many of those people will eventually prefer baseball?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{7}$ of the people will prefer baseball. Therefore, the number of people who will prefer baseball is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot 1\\,260=360\\,\\text{people}.\n$$"}]}
